In .NET MAUI Shell, you can override `OnBackButtonPressed` and customize the toolbar. Here's a working approach:

**1. Override back button behavior in your page:**

```csharp
public partial class EditProductPage : ContentPage
{
    private bool _hasUnsavedChanges;

    protected override bool OnBackButtonPressed()
    {
        if (_hasUnsavedChanges)
        {
            // Fire-and-forget since the override is synchronous
            ShowUnsavedChangesDialog();
            return true; // Cancel default navigation
        }
        return base.OnBackButtonPressed();
    }

    private async void ShowUnsavedChangesDialog()
    {
        bool leave = await DisplayAlert(
            "Unsaved Changes",
            "You have unsaved changes. Discard and go back?",
            "Discard", "Stay");

        if (leave)
            await Shell.Current.GoToAsync("..");
    }
}
```

**2. Add a custom "Cancel" toolbar button in XAML:**

```xml
<ContentPage xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/dotnet/2021/maui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2009/xaml"
             x:Class="MyApp.EditProductPage">

    <Shell.BackButtonBehavior>
        <BackButtonBehavior
            Command="{Binding CancelCommand}"
            TextOverride="Cancel"
            IconOverride="" />
    </Shell.BackButtonBehavior>

    <!-- page content -->
</ContentPage>
```

**3. Wire up the command in your ViewModel (or code-behind):**

```csharp
// In ViewModel
public ICommand CancelCommand { get; }

public EditProductViewModel()
{
    CancelCommand = new Command(async () =>
    {
        if (HasUnsavedChanges)
        {
            bool leave = await Shell.Current.DisplayAlert(
                "Unsaved Changes",
                "You have unsaved changes. Discard and go back?",
                "Discard", "Stay");

            if (!leave) return;
        }
        await Shell.Current.GoToAsync("..");
    });
}
```

**Key points:**

- `OnBackButtonPressed` handles the **hardware/OS back** button (Android back gesture). Return `true` to cancel navigation.
- `Shell.BackButtonBehavior` handles the **Shell toolbar back** button — it replaces the icon/text and routes taps through your command.
- You need both because they're independent code paths.
- Set `IconOverride=""` (empty string) to hide the arrow and show only the "Cancel" text.
